+// Note [K VNNI storage]
+// ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+// With TRON_K_VNNI (CMake option, default OFF, requires TRON_AMX_DISPATCH) the
+// K plane of every 128-dimension head holds the VNNI pair-interleaved layout of
+// h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp instead of one row per token. The bytes and the
+// page capacity are the same. Only the order of the values changes. The gate
+// is k_vnni::layout_on<head_size>, a property of the storage geometry. So
+// every reader and writer of a slot sees the same layout, whatever the query
+// type and whether AMX (Intel Advanced Matrix Extensions) is available at run
+// time. V is unchanged. Its pair-interleaved layout is already the AMX B
+// operand (the second matrix of the tile multiply).
+//
+// The plane is typed. kv_block::k is a k_vnni_tensor (the owner of the 16 KiB
+// plane) for a VNNI slot and a bf16 array for a row-major slot. The plane is
+// reached only through the owner's views (k_vnni_view) and the typed
+// operations of h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp (store_row, load_row, store_block,
+// copy_token). No raw plane pointer leaves the cache. detail::k_vnni_access is
+// the one route from a view to the plane address, for those operations, the
+// two score kernels and the test-only book::fill_random. That mirrors the
+// packed V plane (Note [VNNI Packed V layout]).
+//
+// Why: the AMX QK kernel reads the VNNI plane directly as its B operand. It
+// computes the scores S = Q . K^T in the orientation the softmax wants
+// (qk_vnni_128x4). So the transposing store of the row-major kernel
+// (Note [QK orientation] in amx_attn_iface.hpp) is not needed.
+//
+// A block holds the VNNI layout only once it is complete and converted. A
+// 16-token block of the plane (page offsets 16 c .. 16 c + 15) holds its rows
+// row-major inside the same 4 KiB (Note [Row-major tail block] in
+// h/tron/tensor/k_vnni.hpp)
+// until all 16 of its K rows are saved and the block has been converted in
+// place. The page records two things per storage slot: the saved rows
+// (k_rows_saved_, one bit per offset) and the converted blocks
+// (k_vnni_blocks_, one bit per block). A converted block always has its 16
+// rows saved; a complete block may wait for its conversion. Every reader and
+// writer of a row takes the layout from the converted-block bit.
+//   - save_k (model.hpp) sets a row's bit after the row is stored, on the main
+//     thread. A block completed that way is queued (state::pending_k_blocks_)
+//     and converted at the forward end (convert_pending_k_blocks, after the
+//     plugin joined its workers and the cooperative GOF staging drained),
+//     unless the hardware attention scores the operation's K (Note [Row-major
+//     blocks under hardware attention] below: no bit, no conversion). Why
+//     not in save_k: a block may be split across two minibatches of one pass
+//     (grains of 8 tokens), and the cooperative GOF staging gather of the
+//     earlier minibatch's tokens (help_drain_gof on the attention workers,
+//     wait_for_coop_gof on main) reads the block's rows row-major while the
+//     later minibatch saves the block's last rows. The attention workers
+//     themselves are not that reader: a pending page is read only after every
+//     minibatch that writes it counted its latch down. The forward end, after
+//     wait_for_coop_gof, is the first point where no reader of this pass is
+//     left. The in-place transposes of clear_kv_complete_at and copy_from rely
+//     on the same rule as the rest of the tree work: no forward pass reads the
+//     book meanwhile (the tree lock is held; the OOM rescue's append shares
+//     this reliance with the book relocation it already does).
+//   - a full-block save (page::set_k_block) writes the VNNI layout directly
+//     and sets no bit. The caller (store_k_block in model.hpp) stores every
+//     KV head first and then publishes the 16 row bits and the block bit with
+//     note_k_block_saved. The bits are per slot, not per KV head, so a reader
+//     must not see the block bit before every head holds the layout. Any
+//     thread of a shared window may do this save (Note [Shared block save]
+//     in model.hpp): every row of that block belongs to that one run.
+//   - clear_kv_complete_at (a new token takes a page offset) drops the row bits
+//     of that offset and of every offset above it (KV entries are appended, so
+//     the offsets above a newly assigned one are free) and the block bits of
+//     those blocks. A converted block that keeps live rows below the offset is
+//     converted back to row-major first, so those rows stay readable. When the
+//     book's storage is not readable (a KV restore not yet published, or
+//     reclaimed storage) only the bits change: the bytes are not valid anyway.
+//   - page copies (copy_from) carry both bit sets with the rows.
+// Why the transition is at 16 saved rows: in decode every step saves one token
+// per user, and the tail block of every user is read again on every step. The
+// row-major tail block costs 4 whole lines per token to store, to score and to
+// stage for the FPGA, where the VNNI column cost 64 lines each (issue #4500).
+// The VNNI blocks are what the AMX kernel needs. A page is dense for that
+// kernel only when all its 64 rows are saved; it is VNNI from the forward end
+// of the pass that completed its last block (at once when that block was a
+// whole-block save), and takes the software loop until then.
+//
+// Note [Row-major blocks under hardware attention]
+// ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+// When the hardware attention scores an operation's K (the operation's
+// cached_operation_uses_hw in model.hpp, decided once per model), save_k
+// records no row bits for the rows a short run stores and queues no block
+// for the forward-end conversion. Such a block stays row-major: no bit is
+// ever set for it, so a page copy (copy_from) does not convert it either.
+// Why: once the sequence has passed the engagement point
+// (hw_attn_engagement_point in model.hpp, 127 by default) the FPGA holds
+// the K/V of every DMA-complete GOF (a group of four consecutive tokens
+// whose rows the staging, gof::populate, copies into a buffer that DMA,
+// direct memory access, moves to the card), and the CPU scores only the
+// tail above them: a page the hardware covers takes the software loop with
+// no K bytes read, and a tail token is scored row by row when it lies in a
+// row-major block (by the VNNI reader qk_group when it lies in a
+// whole-block-saved or converted block). So the AMX kernel never reads a
+// block that decode completes there. What the conversion would cost is a
+// serial main-thread copy of every completed block of every layer and KV
+// head at the forward end (8 KiB of plane traffic plus an 8 KiB stack round
+// trip per block and KV head, every 16th step for every user) and a 64-line
+// gather per row whenever the staging copies a converted block.
+// Whole-block saves (prefill) still store the VNNI layout at once: a page
+// the hardware does not cover yet (before the engagement point, in the
+// first forwards of a prompt, or when the DMA is behind by a page) is
+// dense for the AMX kernel only through them.
+// The cost accepted: a range that the software scores although the
+// operation is a hardware one (before the engagement point, a prompt
+// shorter than 128 tokens, a shard without HBM, USE_HW_ATTN=N raising the
+// engagement point) scores the blocks decode completed with the row-major
+// dotter, where the conversion would have let the AMX kernel score a page
+// of them. Every reader takes its layout from the block bit, and no stored
+// token is missed. Both layouts hold the same K values. The fp32 (32-bit
+// float) add order of the row-major dotter and of k_vnni::qk_group differs
+// (the numerics contract in Note [AMX attention dispatch] in
+// amx_attn_iface.hpp). A score can differ by that add order alone, and so
+// can a generated token.
+//
+// Consumers of a VNNI slot, and how each one changes:
+//   1. save_k (model.hpp) stores K with page::set_k_block when a run of the
+//      pass's tokens fills one whole 16-token block of a page. The 16 rows are
+//      transposed in registers. Every 64-byte pair row of the four panels (a
+//      panel: the 16 pair rows of one 32-dimension step for one 16-token
+//      block, 1 KiB) is written whole (store_block). Any shorter run
+//      (decode: one token per user per step; the last block of a prompt) is
+//      stored row by row with page::set_k_row: 4 whole 64-byte lines per
+//      token into the row-major tail block (store_row_major), the same store
+//      the row-major layout makes. When a pass holds more than 16 tokens the
+//      store may be shared with the idle main helpers (Note [Shared block
+//      save] in model.hpp). TRON_AMX_DISABLE leaves both store paths
+//      untouched.
+//   2. the dense AMX page path (self_attention.hpp) passes page::k_packed, the
+//      read-only view of the plane, to qk_vnni_128x4 when page::k_vnni_blocks
+//      says every block of the page is converted. A page whose last block
+//      waits for the forward end, a page with a block that stays row-major
+//      under hardware attention, or a page filled by another writer than
+//      save_k, takes the software loop.
+//   3. the software path for partial pages, the kill switch
+//      (TRON_AMX_DISABLE=1) and hosts without AMX read the VNNI blocks with
+//      k_vnni::qk_group, with the same view. That reader is AVX-512 code with
+//      one token per vector lane. It needs no per-token gather and no
+//      horizontal reduction. The tokens of the row-major tail block are
+//      scored with the row-major dotter (the per-token dot-product loop of
+//      self_attention.hpp) through their typed row (row_major_row), 4 lines
+//      per token.
+//   4. the FPGA (field-programmable gate array) staging path (gof::populate ->
+//      shuffle_k_entry) needs one row per token. For a row-major block (the
+//      usual case under hardware attention, Note [Row-major blocks under
+//      hardware attention]) page_info::k_head_fn returns the row pointer from
+//      page::k_row_if_row_major. No copy is made. For a converted block it
+//      gathers the row into caller-owned scratch with page::get_k_row.
+//      v_head_fn copies the V row into caller-owned scratch when
+//      TRON_CHUNK_SIZE is 16, and returns page::v_ptr in place otherwise. The
+//      staging bytes and the HBM (High Bandwidth Memory, the FPGA device
+//      memory) layout are unchanged.
+//   5. page copies (copy_storage_slot: append and defrag) copy a whole page
+//      as one owner assignment (dst_block.k = src_block.k) when the range
+//      covers the page, bits included. A token's position inside the page
+//      decides its column. So every other range moves token by token: the
+//      converted destination blocks the range touches are made row-major
+//      first (block_to_rows), the rows are copied row-major, and a block
+//      whose 16 rows are saved after the copy is converted to VNNI
+//      (block_to_vnni).
+//   6. the row view page::k() does not exist for VNNI slots. A token's values
+//      are not contiguous there. The layout-agnostic set_k_row / get_k_row
+//      accessors exist for every slot. They take one token's row as a typed
+//      view (const_k_row_view / k_row_view) and follow the block's recorded
+//      layout.
+// The kill switch keeps the layout. With TRON_AMX_DISABLE=1 a TRON_K_VNNI
+// binary runs the AVX-512 VNNI reader, not the row-major dotter. So it is an
+// AMX-off run of the same layout, not a rollback to the row-major binary's
+// numerics. The fp32 (32-bit float) add order of the two readers differs (see
+// the numerics contract in Note [AMX attention dispatch]).
+
